Applications - membership by bookmark
description: filing a node or fragment under an onto is one & line - parameters live on the fragment
- >
  Applying an [onto](*::annotations:ontos) to a target is **one bookmark line** on the target -
  a membership edge, nothing more:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  A Pinch of Math
  &:: ontos: genre: brevity: shortest-paper: -
- >
  The **whole node** carries its own memberships; a **fragment** carries memberships for just
  its region - in marklower prose, right inside the [fragment
  token](*::documents:marklower:grammar):
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  Mathematics likes to hide, but a standalone [&:: yamlover: ontos: topic: math: -: LaTeX]() block
  gives it away.
- >
  An application **carries no data of its own** - an edge cannot. Whatever the old parametrized
  application said (`description`, `importance`, ...) lives on the **fragment**, which is an
  open node ([fragments](*::annotations:fragments)); a *whole-node* application that needs
  parameters targets a **selector-less fragment** - the fragment that stands for the whole
  node - and puts them there.
- >
  The applied onto's **color** (its explicit `color`, else a name-derived hue) drives display -
  never the selector. Two ontos on one target = two bookmark lines; membership is additive, and
  the onto sees its members in bookmark order after its own entries
  ([bookmarks](*::language:pointers:bookmarks)).
